course: everyday
lesson: 436
title: Covering for a friend
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ben's mate Jamie kicked a ball through the classroom window in Liverpool. The teacher asked Ben if he'd seen who. Jamie whispered: «Sound, don't grass, lad» — «sound» means «you're all right», and to grass means to tell on someone. Ben said «I didn't see.» The camera showed Jamie — and Ben standing right next to him. The teacher was disappointed in Ben too. Ben said later he wished he'd just said «Ask Jamie, sir.»
explain: Covering means lying or staying quiet to protect a friend. You don't have to tell on a friend for every small thing, but lying for them is different, and it often drags you in. Sam said he and Ollie «did the homework together» when Ollie had copied; the teacher made them both redo it, though Sam's work had been fine. Josh said Liam's phone in class was his; the teacher took Josh's phone for the day, and Liam didn't even say thanks. Ella agreed to say Niamh was at her house; Niamh was really in town with older kids, and when the mums talked, both girls were in trouble.
explain: Some covering is about real wrong. Grace in Toronto saw Ava take a chocolate bar without paying and told the shopkeeper «We didn't take anything, eh.» The shop had a camera and called both mums; Grace felt sick for days. «I didn't take anything» — said only about yourself — would have been honest. Leo's friend pushed a younger boy over in the park and Leo told his mum «just a game», but the boy had a cut lip. When his dad came round, Leo saw the cut and told the truth. When someone has been hurt, covering is never right.
explain: Silence can cover too. When nobody in a Belfast class would say that Darragh wrote a rude word on the board, the whole class missed break for a week, until Darragh said «It was me, let them go.» Mia knew who put glitter in the teacher's drawer and said «I don't know anything», which was technically true because she hadn't seen it; it never came out, but she felt weird every time the teacher found glitter. Kiri in Rotorua said nothing when her mum asked about the storm; she and her friend had gone to the creek, which flooded. Weeks later Mum found muddy jandals (flip-flops) with creek weed on them, and told her the creek had swept a dog away that day.
explain: Not all help is lying. Alfie lent his friend his own PE kit and sat out PE himself. Nobody lied about anything; Alfie just took the trouble, and his friend bought him a doughnut. Helping a friend with your own sacrifice is different from lying for them — know the difference.
fact: Covering means lying or staying silent to protect someone who did something wrong.
fact: You do not have to report every small thing a friend does, but you should not lie for them.
fact: «Ask him yourself» or «I don't want to say» is honest; «I didn't see» when you did is a lie.
fact: Lying for a friend often gets you into the same trouble, the friend may not even thank you, and the friend learns nothing.
fact: Giving a false alibi about where someone was can put them in danger if something goes wrong.
fact: Lying for someone who stole makes you part of it; speak only for yourself.
fact: When someone has been hurt, covering is never right, because the hurt person needs grown-ups to know.
fact: When a whole group stays silent, everyone may be punished, and the person who did it can end it by owning up.
fact: Saying something technically true to hide the truth is still a kind of trick.
fact: A secret about danger, like playing by a flooding creek, is not yours to keep.
fact: Helping a friend at your own cost, like lending your kit, is kindness, not lying.
fact: To «grass» (UK) means to tell on someone; «sound» (Liverpool, Ireland) means good or all right; «jandals» (NZ) are flip-flops; Canadians often end a sentence with «eh», meaning «right?».
exercise: What did Ben tell the teacher about the window? | «I didn't see» | «It was Jamie, sir» | «I did it, sir» | «Ask Jamie, sir»
exercise: How was the truth about the window found out? | The camera showed Jamie, with Ben next to him | Jamie owned up at once in front of the class | Ben's mum rang the teacher and told her | The ball had Jamie's name written on it
exercise: What did Ben wish he had said? | «Ask Jamie, sir» | «It was a bird» | «I kicked it» | «I wasn't at school»
exercise: Where had Niamh really been when Ella covered for her? | In the town centre with older kids | At Ella's house all day long | At her gran's for tea | At football practice after school
exercise: What happened after Sam said he and Ollie did the homework together? | The teacher made them both redo it | Sam got extra marks for helping | Only Ollie was punished for copying | Nothing happened
exercise: What did Grace say to the shopkeeper? | «We didn't take anything, eh» | «Ava took it, sorry» | «I'll pay for it» | «Call my mum»
exercise: What would have been an honest line for Grace? | «I didn't take anything» — only about herself | «Nobody took anything, I promise» | «It was a mistake by the shop, the till is wrong» | «We paid at the till»
exercise: What happened to Josh for covering for Liam's phone? | His own phone was taken for the day | Liam's phone was taken for a whole week | The teacher thanked him | Both phones were given back
exercise: Why did the whole class in Belfast miss break? | Nobody would say who wrote the rude word | The teacher was ill and nobody could watch them | It was raining all week | Darragh told on everyone
exercise: How did the class's punishment end? | Darragh said «It was me, let them go» | The teacher forgot all about it after a day | The class voted to stop it | A camera showed who did it
exercise: Mia said «I don't know anything» about the glitter. Why was that a trick? | It was technically true but hid what she knew | It was a total lie from start to finish | It was the whole truth | The glitter was hers
exercise: What did Leo tell his mum about the fight in the park? | «Just a game» | «He hit my friend first» | «I don't know» | «A dog pushed him»
exercise: What made Leo tell the truth? | He saw the younger boy's cut lip | His friend told him he should | His mum read the messages on his phone | He was paid to tell
exercise: How was Alfie's help different from covering? | He gave up his own kit and lied about nothing | He lied to the teacher to keep his friend out of trouble | He took his friend's punishment | He hid his friend's mistake
exercise: How did Kiri's mum find out about the creek? | Muddy jandals with creek weed | Kiri's friend told her mum | A neighbour saw them by the water | The local news showed them by the creek
exercise: Why was Kiri's silence dangerous? | The creek had flooded and swept a dog away | The creek was on private farm land | They missed dinner | Her new jandals got ruined in the mud
test: Jamie in Liverpool: «Sound, don't grass, lad». What does «grass» mean? | Tell on someone | Play on a field | Cut the lawn | Run away
test: Mate: «if anyone asks, I was at yours Saturday, yeah?» What's the honest answer? | «I won't lie for you, sorry» | «Sure, no problem, mate» | «Only if you pay me for it» | «Yes, but only to my mum»
test: Ollie copied Sam's work. Teacher: «Why are these identical?» What's honest and fair? | «I let him look» | «We did it together» | «It's a coincidence» | «He copied, I didn't know»
test: Kid in Toronto: «we didn't take anything, eh?» — her friend did. What does «eh» do here? | Asks «right?» at the end | Means she's very tired | Means «no» | Means «hello»
test: Liam: «cheers for taking the blame, I'll do the same for u». What usually happens? | The friend learns nothing and you may be next | The friend always pays you back the next time | You get a medal for it | The teacher thanks you
test: What would have happened if Darragh had owned up on day one? | The class wouldn't have missed a week of break | The whole class would have missed even more break | Nobody would believe him | The teacher would give a prize
test: Leo's friend: «don't tell your mum, it was just a game». The younger boy has a cut lip. What should Leo do? | Tell the truth, because someone was hurt | Keep quiet, because his friend asked him to | Say the younger boy fell | Say he didn't see
test: Alfie lends his PE kit and sits out. Is he lying? | No, he's helping at his own cost | Yes, it's a big lie to the teacher | Yes, covering for anyone is always lying | No, because PE doesn't really matter
test: Kiri and her mate went to a flooding creek and said they «stayed inside». What should Kiri tell her mum? | The truth, because it's about danger | Nothing, because it's all over now | That they stayed inside | That her friend went there alone
test: In a film the hero never «grasses» on his gang, whatever they do. What is true in real life? | Covering for someone who hurt or stole drags you in | Loyalty means lying for your friends about anything | Grown-ups never find out | Covering always protects your friends
test: Mia knows who did the glitter prank but didn't see it. What's an honest line for a small prank? | «I don't want to say who» | «I don't know anything» | «It was the cleaner, Miss» | «It was me»
exam: Identify the statement most consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil who denies having witnessed an act he did witness, in order to protect a friend. | The denial is a falsehood which may implicate the pupil himself | The denial is honest, being made from loyalty to a friend | The denial is required by friendship | The denial bears no risk whatever to the pupil
exam: Select the response appropriate where a friend has caused bodily injury to another child. | Covering is never appropriate, as the injured party requires adult knowledge | Covering is appropriate if the friend so requests | Covering is appropriate if the injury is slight and the friend has apologised | Covering is appropriate where the act was a game
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises collective silence by a class concerning the author of misconduct? | It may result in the punishment of the entire class | It ensures that no person is punished | It is the conduct preferred by teachers | It discloses the author immediately
exam: Determine which of the following does NOT constitute covering for a friend. | The lending of one's own equipment at one's own cost | The provision of a false account of a friend's whereabouts | The denial of misconduct which the pupil has himself witnessed | The assertion that a friend's copied work was done jointly
